# Goal
mohamadreza.com launches as the home of .intent, where every page is itself a verified .intent file and nothing I think about every day stays only in my head.

# Evals
- test: the site builds → npm run build
- test: the homepage has a raw .intent twin → test -f dist/index.html && test -f dist/index.intent
- test: canonical domain is mohamadreza.com → grep -q 'site: "https://mohamadreza.com"' site/astro.config.mjs
- test: CI runs Node 22 or newer → grep -Eq 'node-version: (2[2-9]|[3-9][0-9])' .github/workflows/deploy.yml
- judge: a stranger landing on the homepage understands within 10 seconds that the page they are reading is itself an .intent file
- judge: no eval, page or tool option names a specific AI provider or model
- judge: the launch post shares only the core idea, plainly, and leaves the details open for readers to shape
- review: the site feels like mine, not a template
- review: the launch essay says what I actually think, in my own words
- watch: posts get readers from hosts I never shared them on

# Fulfillment

## The idea in one paragraph
Every era of digital documents froze one layer. PDF locks everything: appearance and content, nothing is editable. Markdown locks nothing: the text is free and each renderer decides the look. .intent is the middle ground: it locks the goal, and the rest of the file stays editable as long as it still meets that goal. Evals are what it takes to meet the goal.

## Two kinds of computers
- `test:` evals are shell commands. Any ordinary computer can run them with ordinary software. No AI needed.
- `judge:` evals are plain-language instructions. Only an AI-assisted computer can run them, using whatever assistant it has. The format never names an API, provider or model.
- `review:` evals are signed off by a human (the author, by default).
- `watch:` evals measure the outside world (reach, traffic, adoption). They are shown but never block. A watch that stays red is a signal to write a new version.
- A file can be partly verified. A plain computer reports e.g. "3/3 tests pass, 2 judges need an AI-assisted computer, 1 review pending". That is a valid state, not an error.
- How a tool treats shell evals from untrusted files is deliberately left out of the spec.

## Writing tests
The syntax is flexible and open to more forms later. All of these are valid:
- one line: `- test: npm run build`
- named: `- test: builds → npm run build` (the name is what readers see)
- fenced block: a bullet with a name, followed by an indented ```sh block for longer scripts

## The lock
- Every file carries `lock: sha256:…`, a hash of its goal and evals, so a changed goal is detectable from the file alone, without git. Verify fails on a missing or mismatched lock.
- `intent lock` writes it. A committed version can't be re-locked with a different goal or evals; `intent lock --bump` makes the next version.
- The agent running a fulfillment treats goal and evals as read-only, so it can't reach the goal by weakening the test.
- Not adopted: goal and evals in front matter (they stay readable sections), hashes in `supersedes`, pinned rubrics for judges.

## Results
- Verdicts live in a fourth section, `# Results`, at the bottom of the file, with date and written reason per eval.
- Like Fulfillment, Results is not locked: recording a verdict never requires a new version.

## Failing evals block
- If any `test:`, `judge:` or `review:` fails, the deploy is blocked.
- A failure means the work no longer meets the locked goal. There are two ways forward: change the fulfillment until it passes, or write a new version with a new goal and evals. Either way the evolution stays in the history.
- `watch:` evals never block.
- Pending evals (○) never block: a review not yet done, or a judge a plain computer can't run, publishes with ○ shown in the margin. Only ✗ blocks.

## The site
- The blog is the home of .intent: spec, launch story and (later) the tool all live on mohamadreza.com.
- Every page is a .intent file. Readers see its goal, eval results and version.
- Raw source is always available: a "view source" toggle on the page, and a twin URL (`/posts/launch` and `/posts/launch.intent`).
- Versions: when a goal or evals change, the page becomes v2 with `supersedes: name@1`. v1 keeps a permanent URL. v2 links to it only through the `supersedes` line in its meta; the old goal is not repeated on the page. v2 re-runs v1's evals against the new text.
- Eval results: tests run in CI on every deploy. Judge and review verdicts are recorded once at publish time in the file's `# Results` section.

## Homepage
- The homepage is a live .intent file.
- Goal: "Nothing I think about every day stays only in my head." (v2; v1 was "Each post finds readers I've never met." and stays at /v1.)
- Shipping cadence is a `watch:` eval (something new ships at least once a week, here or anywhere else), so a quiet week never blocks a deploy. Posts here can be counted from their dates; how to count things shipped elsewhere is still open.

## Post evals
Posts mix four kinds of checks:
- craft (tests): length, links resolve, reading time
- argument (judges): "a skeptic could restate my strongest point", "every claim is sourced"
- reader metrics (watches against analytics): e.g. referrers from new hosts
- personal honesty (reviews): judged by me, never by AI

## Look
- Personal editorial. Serif body text, evals as small monospace margin notes with ✓ / ✗ / ○ and the written reason.
- The headline is the file's `title` if it has one, with the locked goal shown right under it. Without a title, the goal is the headline. The fulfillment is the body.
- Why: a goal is a contract, written to be checked, so it reads like a spec. A title is written for strangers who land on the page.
- Mobile: margin notes collapse into a block under the goal.

## Topics beyond .intent
Format and spec design, AI agents and workflows, software engineering, personal essays.

## Tech
- Monorepo in this repo, npm workspaces:
  - `/spec`: the formal spec (itself a .intent file)
  - `/tool`: TypeScript parser and `intent verify` CLI
  - `/site`: Astro 5, with a content loader that reads .intent files through `/tool`
- Deploy stays GitHub Pages via `.github/workflows/deploy.yml`, adjusted to build the `/site` workspace. Bump Node 20 to 22.
- Fix `site` in the Astro config to `https://mohamadreza.com`.
- Readers download almost no JavaScript: a few inline lines for the light/dark switch, plus the analytics script once it's enabled. "View source" works without JavaScript.
- Theme follows the device by default; the header switch overrides it and is remembered in the browser.

## Launch day
1. Homepage (.intent)
2. Spec page
3. Launch essay: Claude writes headings and placeholders, I write the words.

Examples gallery and an installable tool come after launch.

## Launch essay
- v1 was the .intent vision essay, framed around PDF and Markdown. It stays readable at /posts/launch/v1.
- v2 (title "Lock the goal. Every agent run meets it.") drops the PDF and Markdown history. It introduces .intent as a research result, in the plain, declarative voice of a format announcement: a file has a name, a goal and a fulfillment; an agent reads the fulfillment and produces a result for the goal; evals say what result counts.
- It covers locking the goal while the work stays free, the two kinds of computers, and versions. Short and easy to read, with minimal evals.

# Results
Nothing built yet, so no verdicts recorded.
